<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<CustomField xmlns="http://soap.sforce.com/2006/04/metadata">
    <!--
        This comment must stay INSIDE the root <CustomField> element - a comment placed ABOVE the
        root breaks `sf` deploy at source conversion with "unable to find matching parent xml
        file".

        🔴 USER DECISION 2026-08-16 (post-reversal follow-up, option 2 of the three candidates this
        comment used to list) - THE FORMULA IS NOW RECORD-TYPE AWARE AND BLANKS ON TERMINAL STATES.
        The pre-reversal formula (TODAY() - Date_Sent__c) counted days an NDA had been out awaiting
        signature, back when 'Sent' was a MIDDLE step on the acquisition sequence
        (Pending -> Sent -> Received -> Signed). The sequence reversed to
        Pending -> Received -> Signed -> Sent with 'Sent' now TERMINAL there, so the old formula
        would have counted days since the NDA was FINISHED, growing forever. A flat status
        exclusion cannot fix this: 'Sent' is terminal on Acquisition_NDA but a genuine WAITING
        state on Disposition_NDA (Not Sent -> Sent -> Signed, plus the Declined off-ramp), so the
        same status value means opposite things depending on record type - the formula must branch
        on RecordType.DeveloperName, not on Status__c alone.

        The new formula returns blank once the NDA has reached ITS OWN record type's terminal
        state - 'Sent' for Acquisition_NDA, 'Signed' OR 'Declined' for Disposition_NDA - and
        computes TODAY() - Date_Sent__c only while genuinely outstanding.

        🔴 COUNTER-INTUITIVE CONSEQUENCE, STATED PLAINLY SO IT IS NOT MISTAKEN FOR A BUG: on
        Acquisition_NDA the ONLY status that ever populates Date_Sent__c is 'Sent' itself (see the
        Sent step's <fieldNames> in pathAssistants/NDA_Path_Acquisition), and 'Sent' is now
        terminal - so Days_Out__c IS PERMANENTLY BLANK FOR EVERY ACQUISITION NDA after this change.
        That is the CORRECT outcome under the reversed process: nothing is out awaiting return
        anymore; the waiting period is now "the broker's NDA has not arrived yet", which has no
        stamped date until Date_Received__c is populated (added in this same follow-up - see that
        field's own comment) and this field was never re-pointed at it (candidate remedy 1 was not
        chosen; remedy 2, restriction, was). The field remains fully meaningful on Disposition_NDA,
        where 'Sent' is a real waiting state and the days-out count still runs until Signed or
        Declined.

        ⚠ REPORT CONSEQUENCE: reports/Acquisitions/NDA_Tracker_Recent will now show a blank
        Days_Out__c for every acquisition row. This is INTENDED and strictly better than the
        growing-forever number the pre-fix formula would have shown for a completed NDA - do not
        "fix" the report's blankness back without revisiting this decision first.

        🔴 CORRECTION 2026-08-16 (SAME-DAY, POST-REVIEW) - THE FIRST VERSION OF THIS FORMULA HAD NO
        BRANCH FOR A BLANK / MASTER RECORD TYPE, AND THAT WAS THE CASE THAT ACTUALLY MATTERED. It
        tested RecordType.DeveloperName = "Acquisition_NDA" and = "Disposition_NDA" only. A row
        whose RecordTypeId is null or Master matches NEITHER, so the OR was false and the formula
        fell through to TODAY() - Date_Sent__c regardless. RecordStageAdvanceService.sequenceFor /
        sequenceKeyFor (see that class) treats a null/Master record type as ACQUISITION via
        StageConfig.defaultTypeKey, and its own header calls that fallback "LOAD-BEARING, NOT
        DEFENSIVE": between the record-type deploy (T1) and the row migration (gate T-B), every
        live NDA in the org sits on Master. Left unfixed, this field's restriction would have done
        nothing on today's real data - Days_Out__c would keep growing forever on precisely the
        acquisition NDAs this change exists to fix, while the repo looked correct and the org was
        unchanged. Confirmed empirically (not merely reasoned) 2026-08-16: SELECT Id,
        DeveloperName, Name FROM RecordType WHERE SobjectType = 'NDA__c' against usman-dpeg returns
        only Acquisition_NDA and Disposition_NDA - the Master pseudo-type has no queryable row on
        this object, so a record with RecordTypeId null OR pointed at Master resolves
        RecordType.DeveloperName to BLANK either way. ISBLANK(RecordType.DeveloperName) below is
        therefore the correct and sufficient test for "this row has no real record type yet" - no
        separate literal "Master" string comparison is needed or added.

        THE FIX TREATS BLANK RECORD TYPE AS ACQUISITION, TO MATCH
        RecordStageAdvanceService.defaultTypeKey - NAMED HERE ON PURPOSE SO THE TWO CANNOT DRIFT
        APART WITHOUT SOMEONE NOTICING. If that service's fallback is ever repointed to a different
        default (e.g. if the disposition side becomes the majority-live-data type), THIS FORMULA
        MUST MOVE WITH IT - grep this repo for "defaultTypeKey" before changing either one alone.

        ⚠ THIS COUPLING IS TEMPORARY IN INTENT, NOT IN ENFORCEMENT. It exists only because of the
        un-run row migration (gate T-B) that RecordStageAdvanceService's own header describes -
        once every live NDA carries a real record type, the blank/Master branch below becomes
        unreachable on live data and could in principle be retired. But nothing in this repo
        guarantees WHEN that migration runs, so the formula must keep handling a blank record type
        correctly regardless of whether that day has arrived - this is not a stopgap that is safe
        to leave half-done.

        ⚠ KNOWN, DELIBERATE ASYMMETRY - DO NOT "FIX" IT HERE. A Master-type DISPOSITION row (should
        one ever exist) is treated as ACQUISITION by this formula, exactly as
        RecordStageAdvanceService already treats it. That is a pre-existing consequence of the
        SHARED fallback documented in that class's header, not a new decision made here, and
        matching it - rather than inventing a different, field-local rule - is the point. A
        Master-type disposition NDA is architecturally supposed to be closed by gate T-A1/T-B, the
        same gate this whole correction is scoped around.

        🔴 SHAPE CHOSEN, ARGUED DELIBERATELY: "Acquisition_NDA OR blank" (enumeration), NOT "NOT
        Disposition_NDA" (inversion). Two candidate shapes were considered:
          - Enumeration ("Acquisition_NDA OR blank") lists the acquisition case explicitly. A
            future THIRD record type on this object (named, real, not yet configured) falls
            through to the OLD, safe TODAY() - Date_Sent__c behavior - it keeps counting rather
            than silently going blank under a rule nobody decided applies to it.
          - Inversion ("NOT Disposition_NDA") makes acquisition the default for everything else,
            silently sweeping a future third record type into the acquisition terminal-blanking
            rule with no one having decided that is correct for it.
        Enumeration was chosen because it matches RecordStageAdvanceService's OWN stated stance in
        the opposite direction: that class explicitly REFUSES (never defaults) "A REAL, NAMED
        record type with no configured sequence" - its header calls a silent fallback there
        "unsafe rather than merely untidy". A formula cannot throw, so it cannot literally refuse -
        but enumeration is the shape that does NOT silently apply the acquisition rule to an
        unconfigured third type, which is the same caution in formula terms: an unrecognised
        record type keeps showing its (possibly stale) day count rather than having it silently
        hidden by a rule that was only ever argued for two named types plus the migration-window
        blank/Master case.
    -->
    <fullName>Days_Out__c</fullName>
    <description>Days since the NDA was sent (TODAY - Date Sent), blank once the NDA reaches its own record type's terminal state (Sent for Acquisition_NDA or blank/Master, matching RecordStageAdvanceService.defaultTypeKey; Signed or Declined for Disposition_NDA). Acquisition and not-yet-migrated (blank/Master) NDAs therefore read permanently blank once Sent - see the field's own comment for why. Gives the dashboard NDA Tracker table a numeric column and flags aging NDAs still genuinely outstanding.</description>
    <externalId>false</externalId>
    <formula><![CDATA[IF(
  ISBLANK(Date_Sent__c),
  null,
  IF(
    OR(
      AND(
        OR(RecordType.DeveloperName = "Acquisition_NDA", ISBLANK(RecordType.DeveloperName)),
        TEXT(Status__c) = "Sent"
      ),
      AND(RecordType.DeveloperName = "Disposition_NDA", TEXT(Status__c) = "Signed"),
      AND(RecordType.DeveloperName = "Disposition_NDA", TEXT(Status__c) = "Declined")
    ),
    null,
    TODAY() - Date_Sent__c
  )
)]]></formula>
    <formulaTreatBlanksAs>BlankAsZero</formulaTreatBlanksAs>
    <label>Days Out</label>
    <precision>18</precision>
    <required>false</required>
    <scale>0</scale>
    <trackHistory>false</trackHistory>
    <trackTrending>false</trackTrending>
    <type>Number</type>
    <unique>false</unique>
</CustomField>
